"The prognosis following robot-assisted radical prostatectomy is favorable based on the documented surgical findings. Key positive factors include a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), negative surgical margins, absence of extraprostatic extension, non-invasion of the seminal vesicles, and no lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was 4.3. Countervailing factors include the missing Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the biopsy, which widens uncertainty regarding the true tumor biology. Nodal status was documented as negative. The current prognostic horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 77 months without an event. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this assessment. Further data on postoperative PSA levels and trends are necessary to refine the long-term outlook. The fixed estimated horizon is 77.09 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 61.68–92.51 months; its coverage has not been validated."
